WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.930
You spin a boat-shaped stone clockwise on a tabletop, and it turns smoothly.

2
00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:14.180
But spin it the other way, and after a few turns it stops, begins to rock all on its own, and finally starts spinning back in the direction it prefers.

3
00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:21.830
How does a stone decide which way to turn, and how does it do so without breaking one of the most fundamental conservation laws in physics?

4
00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:35.600
On the table sits a smooth stone about the size of your palm, shaped a little like a boat.

5
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.410
Its underside is gently curved, its top is flat.

6
00:00:39.410 --> 00:00:43.380
You take it by the edge with your finger and spin it clockwise.

7
00:00:43.380 --> 00:00:48.950
The stone turns a few times, slows down, and then something unexpected happens.

8
00:00:48.950 --> 00:00:55.640
It stops spinning and begins to rock, its nose tipping up and down, rattling against the table.

9
00:00:55.640 --> 00:01:02.970
The rocking lasts a few seconds, and then, all by itself, it reverses and starts spinning counterclockwise.

10
00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:09.180
Nobody touched it, yet the stone rejected the direction you gave it and switched to one of its own choosing.

11
00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:12.510
Now spin the same stone counterclockwise.

12
00:01:12.510 --> 00:01:15.680
This time there's no objection at all.

13
00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:21.250
The stone turns calmly, and it keeps on turning until friction slowly brings it to rest.

14
00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:27.140
It's as if the stone has a preference: it accepts spinning one way, and refuses to spin the other.

15
00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:33.190
In the English-speaking world this object is called a rattleback, after the way its back rattles.

16
00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:36.200
Another name for it is the celt.

17
00:01:36.200 --> 00:01:42.730
In Turkish, too, it's known as the Kelt stone, a name derived from that second term.

18
00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:47.020
But the name is misleading, because it has nothing to do with the Celts.

19
00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:53.710
In archaeology, a celt is the name given to prehistoric axe and chisel heads made of polished stone.

20
00:01:53.710 --> 00:01:58.160
And the story of the word itself is almost as strange as the stone.

21
00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:03.890
For a long time, it was accepted that a Latin word, celtis, meant chisel.

22
00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:09.780
The source cited for this word was a line in the Book of Job, in the Latin translation of the Bible.

23
00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:21.110
Later comparisons, however, revealed that in more reliable manuscripts, the word written at that point was certe, meaning "certainly." In other words,

24
00:02:21.110 --> 00:02:27.320
a single copyist's slip of the pen may have given archaeology a term it would go on using for centuries.

25
00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:38.970
According to the story that has been handed down, in the nineteenth century some researchers studying these polished stone tools noticed that certain specimens, when spun on a flat surface, favored a single direction.

26
00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:45.820
Exactly who made this observation, and where it was first recorded, isn't clear, and here we need to be careful.

27
00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:51.550
There is no evidence whatsoever that prehistoric people deliberately shaped these axes to behave this way.

28
00:02:51.550 --> 00:02:58.240
The behavior was most likely a by-product of the fact that an object shaped by hand can never be perfectly symmetrical.

29
00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:04.610
If the stone held a secret at all, that secret lay not in the knowledge of its maker, but in the stone's geometry.

30
00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:08.260
And that is precisely what caught the attention of physicists.

31
00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:13.670
Because it seemed to shake one of the most basic intuitions of classical mechanics.

32
00:03:13.670 --> 00:03:17.640
A spinning body tends to keep on spinning.

33
00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:24.490
You spin a top, the top slows down and stops, but it never starts turning the other way on its own.

34
00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:29.580
The quantity we call angular momentum is conserved unless something outside acts on it.

35
00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:35.630
The celt, though, seemed to be breaking that rule, reversing its direction of spin all by itself.

36
00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:42.800
The first person to take this puzzle seriously in mathematical terms was the English physicist and mathematician Gilbert Walker.

37
00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:51.730
In a paper published in eighteen ninety-six, Walker set out the equations of motion for this object, which he described as a kind of dynamical top.

38
00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:55.860
Today, Walker's name is remembered mostly in a different field.

39
00:03:55.860 --> 00:04:04.630
He would later go on to head the meteorological service in India, and to carry out groundbreaking work on the great oscillations between the ocean and the atmosphere.

40
00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:13.240
But as a young Cambridge mathematician, he was wrestling with why this stone, small enough to fit in the palm of his hand, behaved so stubbornly.

41
00:04:13.240 --> 00:04:19.130
And the conclusion he reached was this: there is no magic in the stone.

42
00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:25.180
But there is, in its shape, a very subtle twist, one that doesn't catch the eye at first glance.

43
00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:31.550
To understand that twist, we need to think about two different identities of the stone, one at a time.

44
00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:34.880
The first is how the stone's mass is distributed.

45
00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:40.130
A long, flat object has certain axes around which it is easiest, and hardest, to turn.

46
00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:44.420
In physics, these are called the principal axes of inertia.

47
00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:52.390
Think of a long ruler: spinning it around its long axis is easy, but holding it by the middle and spinning it like a propeller takes more effort.

48
00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:57.160
The distribution of mass draws an invisible set of axes onto the body.

49
00:04:57.160 --> 00:05:00.810
The second identity is the shape of the stone's underside.

50
00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:05.580
The base of a celt is curved, like the bottom of an egg or an ellipsoid.

51
00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:08.910
But that curve is not the same in every direction.

52
00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:15.280
Along one direction the curve is sharper, and along the direction at right angles to it, the curve is flatter.

53
00:05:15.280 --> 00:05:19.570
These two directions form the base's own set of axes.

54
00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:25.140
In an ordinary, symmetrical object, these two sets of axes line up exactly.

55
00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:30.870
The long axis of the mass runs along the same line as the long curve of the base.

56
00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:33.720
In a celtic stone, they don't.

57
00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:39.930
There is a small angle between the two sets of axes, often a shift of only a few degrees.

58
00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:46.300
Sometimes this is done with weights placed on top of the stone, set slightly askew from its long axis.

59
00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:50.430
Sometimes it is done by carving the base with a slight twist.

60
00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:53.760
The eye usually can't catch the difference.

61
00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:55.970
But the stone does.

62
00:05:55.970 --> 00:05:59.140
All of its stubbornness comes from this hidden angle.

63
00:05:59.140 --> 00:06:03.590
Now, instead of spinning the stone, let's try something else.

64
00:06:03.590 --> 00:06:09.000
While the stone sits still on the table, press gently on one end with your finger and let go.

65
00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:14.410
The stone starts rocking back and forth like a seesaw, its nose bobbing up and down.

66
00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:16.780
This is called pitching.

67
00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:21.710
On a symmetrical stone, the rocking would simply die away where it started.

68
00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:28.880
On a celtic stone, though, the stone slowly begins to turn while it rocks, and always in one particular direction.

69
00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:36.530
If you press on the side edge instead and tip it from side to side, which is called rolling, most stones will start turning the other way.

70
00:06:36.530 --> 00:06:39.700
The rocking turns into spin.

71
00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:44.470
And the kind of rocking decides which way the stone spins.

72
00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:48.280
Let's follow, step by step, why this happens.

73
00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:54.170
As the stone pitches, the point where it touches the table slides back and forth across the base.

74
00:06:54.170 --> 00:07:04.060
If the base were symmetrical, this contact point would travel exactly along the stone's long axis, on a line running straight in front of and straight behind the center of mass.

75
00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:11.390
But because the curvature axes of the base are turned slightly away from the mass axes, the contact point doesn't move in a straight line.

76
00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:14.400
It moves along a slightly diagonal path.

77
00:07:14.400 --> 00:07:19.330
When the stone tips forward, the contact point shifts a little to the right.

78
00:07:19.330 --> 00:07:26.180
When it tips back, it shifts a little to the left, or the other way around, depending on how the stone is built.

79
00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:29.030
This shift is the key.

80
00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:33.800
The table is pushing on the stone at the contact point at every moment.

81
00:07:33.800 --> 00:07:40.330
That push is made up of an upward force holding the stone up, and a friction force running along the surface of the table.

82
00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:50.860
When these forces act not directly in line with the center of mass but a little to one side, they produce a small twist, a torque, that tries to turn the stone around a vertical axis.

83
00:07:50.860 --> 00:07:57.070
Pushing a door in line with its hinges does nothing, but pushing it at its edge swings it open.

84
00:07:57.070 --> 00:08:02.320
In the same way, the table pushes the stone slightly off center with every rock.

85
00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:05.490
And here is the real subtlety.

86
00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:13.940
If the stone is pushed from the right when it tips forward, and from the left when it tips back, the twists from the two half swings don't cancel out.

87
00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:16.790
They add up in the same direction.

88
00:08:16.790 --> 00:08:21.400
Every rock gives the stone another small nudge of spin in the same direction.

89
00:08:21.400 --> 00:08:26.650
Now let's go back to the very beginning, to the moment we spun the stone the wrong way.

90
00:08:26.650 --> 00:08:36.380
While the stone spins in the direction it doesn't like, there is a link between the spinning and the rocking, caused by that same hidden angle, and this link works in reverse.

91
00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:40.510
The spin feeds small pitching oscillations inside the stone.

92
00:08:40.510 --> 00:08:47.680
The tiny vibrations that are always there, instead of fading away, grow when the stone spins in this direction.

93
00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:52.130
In the language of mathematics, spin in this direction is unstable.

94
00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:57.860
The moment the stone starts to rattle is the moment spin energy begins flowing into the rocking.

95
00:08:57.860 --> 00:09:06.310
As the rocking grows, the mechanism we just described kicks in, and the twist produced by the rocking starts working against the existing spin.

96
00:09:06.310 --> 00:09:12.520
The spin slows, stops, and finally starts up again in the direction the stone prefers.

97
00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:16.010
In this new direction, the link works the other way.

98
00:09:16.010 --> 00:09:20.620
The spin no longer makes the rocking grow, it damps it.

99
00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:24.110
The stone settles down and spins along easily.

100
00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:27.440
So there is no will at work here, only a loop.

101
00:09:27.440 --> 00:09:32.690
Spin in the unwanted direction creates the rocking that brings about its own end.

102
00:09:32.690 --> 00:09:37.620
Spin in the preferred direction suppresses the rocking and keeps itself going.

103
00:09:37.620 --> 00:09:43.670
What we call the stone's stubbornness is nothing more than the geometry treating these two directions unequally.

104
00:09:43.670 --> 00:09:52.600
Although this explanation was laid out in qualitative terms at the end of the nineteenth century, the full details kept physicists busy for a long time.

105
00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:58.970
Walker built a linearized model of the motion for small oscillations and showed which direction was unstable.

106
00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:10.940
But following the whole reversal, that long, nonlinear process in which the stone slows, stops, and switches direction, only became possible once computers came to the rescue with numerical solutions.

107
00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:17.790
Simulations in the nineteen-eighties showed that the behavior seen in real stones came straight out of the equations.

108
00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:26.880
It even turned out that some stones, depending on their design, can be unstable in both directions, meaning that after reversing once, they can reverse yet again.

109
00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:30.690
These stones never settle on one direction.

110
00:10:30.690 --> 00:10:35.780
They go back and forth between the two several times, a little slower each time.

111
00:10:35.780 --> 00:10:40.230
But how does all of this square with the firm rule we stated at the very start?

112
00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:44.360
Wasn't angular momentum supposed to be conserved?

113
00:10:44.360 --> 00:10:49.290
The stone was spinning clockwise, and now it's spinning the other way.

114
00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:52.940
Where did that spin go, and where did the new spin come from?

115
00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:58.190
The answer lies in remembering when the conservation of angular momentum actually applies.

116
00:10:58.190 --> 00:11:04.560
The conservation law holds for an isolated system, one that receives no twisting force from outside.

117
00:11:04.560 --> 00:11:07.570
But the celt stone is not isolated.

118
00:11:07.570 --> 00:11:13.140
At every moment it touches the table, and at every moment the table pushes back against it.

119
00:11:13.140 --> 00:11:19.030
That off-center push we just saw is precisely a twisting force applied to the stone from outside.

120
00:11:19.030 --> 00:11:24.280
So when the stone's own angular momentum changes, no law is being broken.

121
00:11:24.280 --> 00:11:29.530
The conservation law hasn't vanished; it has simply moved to a larger system.

122
00:11:29.530 --> 00:11:39.580
If you consider the stone and the table together, along with the floor the table stands on, the building, and ultimately the entire Earth, the total angular momentum is still conserved.

123
00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:49.630
When the stone, spinning clockwise, slows down and reverses direction, the table applies a twist to it in one direction and receives an equal twist in the opposite direction.

124
00:11:49.630 --> 00:11:53.760
That angular momentum is ultimately passed on to our planet.

125
00:11:53.760 --> 00:12:00.130
The Earth's mass is so enormous that the change this transfer makes to its rotation is far too small to measure.

126
00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:03.780
But in principle, it is there.

127
00:12:03.780 --> 00:12:10.150
Every time a celt stone changes direction, the Earth's rotation shifts by an unimaginably tiny amount.

128
00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:15.720
The law hasn't disappeared; it has simply hidden itself inside the Earth.

129
00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:18.730
There is one more important detail here.

130
00:12:18.730 --> 00:12:26.220
For this mechanism to work, the table has to be able to grip the stone horizontally, which means friction has to be present.

131
00:12:26.220 --> 00:12:33.070
On a perfectly slippery surface, say a flawless ice rink, the table could only push the stone straight up.

132
00:12:33.070 --> 00:12:41.680
And those vertical forces could have only a very limited effect, because producing a twist around a vertical axis requires a force acting along the surface.

133
00:12:41.680 --> 00:12:48.050
Real experiments and calculations show that as friction decreases, the reversal effect grows weaker.

134
00:12:48.050 --> 00:12:54.260
The true driver of the stone's stubbornness is the friction of the table, holding it in place without letting it slide.

135
00:12:54.260 --> 00:12:57.590
But the word friction invites a misunderstanding here.

136
00:12:57.590 --> 00:13:02.840
When we hear friction, most of us think of lost energy, heat, slowing down.

137
00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:09.370
Yet the friction at play in the celt stone is, to a first approximation, the kind that consumes no energy.

138
00:13:09.370 --> 00:13:16.540
As long as the stone rolls across the table without slipping, the point of contact is, for that instant, motionless.

139
00:13:16.540 --> 00:13:22.270
A force applied to a point that doesn't move does no work, which means it spends no energy.

140
00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:28.480
This force only steers the direction of motion, transferring energy from one kind of movement to another.

141
00:13:28.480 --> 00:13:34.690
And this is one of the most surprising layers added to the celt stone story in the late twentieth century.

142
00:13:34.690 --> 00:13:44.900
Hermann Bondi, known for his work in cosmology, took up the problem in a study published in nineteen eighty-six, treating it as a question of the rigid-body dynamics of one-way spinning.

143
00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:54.630
When physicists examined the stone's motion using an idealized model, in which it rolls without ever slipping and without ever losing energy, they found that the reversal still happened.

144
00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:59.560
In other words, the loss of energy is not what causes the change of direction.

145
00:13:59.560 --> 00:14:06.570
The energy of the spin flows into the wobble, and the energy of the wobble flows back into a spin in the opposite direction.

146
00:14:06.570 --> 00:14:11.500
In real stones, of course, some energy does escape as heat and sound.

147
00:14:11.500 --> 00:14:14.510
That clattering noise is exactly that.

148
00:14:14.510 --> 00:14:20.240
But the heart of the story is not energy disappearing; it is energy changing form.

149
00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:24.210
This idealized model has an unexpected side as well.

150
00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:33.140
In physics, we tend to think that systems which conserve energy can usually run backward in time under the same rules, oscillating in a kind of balance.

151
00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:44.150
Systems bound by the condition of rolling without slipping, however, belong to a separate family that physicists call nonholonomic systems, and they don't always obey these familiar rules.

152
00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:56.920
When the Russian mathematicians Alexey Borisov and Ivan Mamaev studied this idealized celt stone model in the early two thousands, they found that even though energy is conserved, the system can display chaotic behavior,

153
00:14:56.920 --> 00:15:02.490
and even strange attractors, which are normally considered a hallmark of systems that lose energy.

154
00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:11.420
This toy that fits in the palm of your hand became, for mathematicians, a laboratory showing that the conservation of energy does not mean order.

155
00:15:11.420 --> 00:15:16.670
The story has one more layer, and this one touches the stone's deepest property.

156
00:15:16.670 --> 00:15:20.320
Imagine placing a celt stone in front of a mirror.

157
00:15:20.320 --> 00:15:24.930
Its reflection is a stone whose hidden angle is skewed the opposite way.

158
00:15:24.930 --> 00:15:30.500
If you were to actually make that mirror-image stone, it would prefer to spin in the other direction.

159
00:15:30.500 --> 00:15:38.630
In other words, the celt stone, just like our right and left hands, is an object that cannot be superimposed on its own mirror image.

160
00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:42.920
Scientists call this chirality, or handedness.

161
00:15:42.920 --> 00:15:53.770
In a study from two thousand eight, the mathematicians Keith Moffatt and Tadashi Tokieda approached the celt stone's reversal from exactly this angle, treating it as a prime example of chiral dynamics.

162
00:15:53.770 --> 00:15:58.380
The stone's preference is the handedness of its shape reflected in its motion.

163
00:15:58.380 --> 00:16:03.630
Neither the table, nor gravity, nor the Earth's rotation favors one direction over the other.

164
00:16:03.630 --> 00:16:07.760
The only thing that chooses the direction is the stone's own asymmetry.

165
00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:14.450
This perspective turns the Celt stone from a simple tabletop toy into a small model of a much broader question.

166
00:16:14.450 --> 00:16:19.060
Nature is full of processes that favor one direction over the other.

167
00:16:19.060 --> 00:16:30.230
In chemistry, the right-handed and left-handed forms of a molecule behave differently; in biology, the proteins of living things are built almost entirely from amino acids of the same handedness.

168
00:16:30.230 --> 00:16:35.960
The Celt stone is not the answer to these great questions, and it would be wrong to claim that it is.

169
00:16:35.960 --> 00:16:47.450
But it shows something very concrete: even when the laws themselves treat both directions equally, a tiny asymmetry in an object's shape can turn into a strikingly clear preference at the level of motion.

170
00:16:47.450 --> 00:16:52.220
Symmetric rules need no great force to produce asymmetric outcomes.

171
00:16:52.220 --> 00:16:55.870
A shift of just a few degrees is enough.

172
00:16:55.870 --> 00:17:04.480
Today, Celt stones are made of plastic, wood, or stone, and they pass from hand to hand in science museums and physics classrooms.

173
00:17:04.480 --> 00:17:09.090
Anyone who sees one for the first time almost always reacts the same way.

174
00:17:09.090 --> 00:17:17.700
First they are surprised, then they look for the trick, wondering whether there is a magnet inside, a hidden motor, or some kind of weighted mechanism.

175
00:17:17.700 --> 00:17:25.350
Learning that the trick is actually in plain sight, in the very shape of the stone, may be the most beautiful lesson this object has to offer.

176
00:17:25.350 --> 00:17:36.520
This journey, which begins with a prehistoric axe head, takes its name from a manuscript error, passes through a meteorologist's notebook, and reaches all the way to the equations of chaos theorists,

177
00:17:36.520 --> 00:17:39.050
arrives at a single point.

178
00:17:39.050 --> 00:17:44.140
Even in its strangest-looking behavior, nature never breaks its own laws.

179
00:17:44.140 --> 00:17:48.270
It simply applies them in a corner we don't immediately notice.

180
00:17:48.270 --> 00:17:52.080
And the stone is still spinning on the table.

181
00:17:52.080 --> 00:17:56.530
Whichever direction you give it, it will eventually turn back to its own.

182
00:17:56.530 --> 00:18:01.140
But now we know that behind this stubbornness there is neither a secret nor a will.

183
00:18:01.140 --> 00:18:08.790
There is only a curved base, a mass sitting slightly turned against it, and a table that quietly pushes it from the edge at every moment.

184
00:18:08.790 --> 00:18:12.280
We've come to the end of today's episode of Nöron Bilim.

185
00:18:12.280 --> 00:18:14.810
Until we meet again in a new story.
